	Everything was going fine until our user disk ran out of space.
A friend of mine attempted to save his awesome game and was informed that
something had gone horribly wrong.  "Contact agrimm -- rebuild may be
possible."  So now I've got angry players yelling at me to use my
limitless game administrator powers and "make things right."  Sigh.

	This is Nethack 3.0j running on an IBM RISC/6000 using AIX UNIX.
What I have to work with are files in both the "playground" and "save"
directories.  They look something like:

	Playground			Save
	----------  			----
	1345Chopper.0			1345Chopper
	1345Chopper.1			1345Chopper.e
	1345Chopper.2
	etc.   

	For all you non-networkers out there, the 1345 is the player's
UID.  The first set of files represent (I presume) each level of the
dungeon, and the second set are the result of a failed file build.  Both
"save" files are equal in length (about 64B).  I have never seen anything
like the 1345Chopper.e file before (maybe it stores the character info?).
Naturally, attempting to play under this name results in a "Game in
progress, delete it?" message.

	So what I need to know is how to rebuild a workable save file from
these pieces, if at all possible.  I'm no C programmer, so please keep any
explanations simple.  Thanks in advance!

